  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "In Texas Dept. of Housing and Community Affairs v. Inclusive Communities Project, Inc. (2015), the Supreme Court held that disparate impact claims are cognizable under the Fair Housing Act, but such claims require plaintiffs to prove that a policy causes a disparate impact and that the policy is not necessary to achieve a valid interest.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-25"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "In Texas Dept. of Housing, the Court emphasized that disparate impact liability must be limited to avoid creating racial quotas and to ensure that liability is not imposed for mere statistical disparities without a causal link to a specific policy.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-25"
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "The Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals, in the case of Inclusive Communities Project, Inc. v. Lincoln Property Co. (2017), affirmed the district court's summary judgment in favor of the respondents, holding that the plaintiffs failed to establish a prima facie case of disparate impact because the statistical disparity was not caused by the specific policies challenged.",
      "source_date": "2017-08-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court granted certiorari in Inclusive Communities Project, Inc. v. Lincoln Property Co. on October 2, 2018, to address whether the Fifth Circuit's application of the disparate impact standard was correct and whether the plaintiffs met the burden of proof required under the Fair Housing Act.",
      "source_date": "2018-10-02"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "In the 2015 decision, the Court noted that the Fair Housing Act's text and history support a disparate impact theory but also stressed that the statute does not mandate quotas and that defendants may defend policies by showing they serve a substantial, legitimate, nondiscriminatory interest.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-25"
