  "reasoning": "The central legal question is whether the Dodd-Frank Act's anti-retaliation provisions extend to individuals who report securities violations internally but not to the SEC. The statutory text of 15 U.S.C. § 78u-6(a)(6) explicitly defines a 'whistleblower' as an individual who provides information 'to the Commission' (the SEC) [7]. Furthermore, the anti-retaliation provision in § 78u-6(h)(1)(A) applies to individuals who 'provide information to the Commission' [2]. This plain language suggests that internal reporting alone is insufficient to trigger protection. The SEC's regulations, which define a whistleblower as someone reporting to the SEC, align with this textual reading [6]. While the Ninth Circuit held that internal reporters are protected [4], this interpretation conflicts with the explicit statutory definition requiring communication with the Commission. Under the principle established in Chevron [9], if the statute is unambiguous, the Court must follow the text rather than defer to an agency interpretation that might expand the scope, or in this case, the Ninth Circuit's expansion. The Supreme Court has consistently applied a strict textualist approach when the statutory language is clear, as seen in X-Citement Video [8]. Since the statute defines the protected class as those reporting to the SEC, and the petitioner argues that internal reporters fall outside this definition, the textual evidence strongly supports the petitioner's position that the Ninth Circuit erred in expanding the definition beyond the statutory text.",
