    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court has historically applied a deferential standard of review to agency interpretations of the Immigration and Nationality Act under the Chevron doctrine, as established in Chevron U.S.A., Inc. v. Natural Resources Defense Council, Inc. (1984), though this deference has been subject to increasing scrutiny in recent years.",
      "source_date": "1984-06-25"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "In 2022, the Supreme Court decided FDA v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, signaling a potential shift in the Court's approach to administrative law, with several justices expressing skepticism about broad agency deference, though the specific holding was limited to standing.",
      "source_date": "2022-06-23"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "The case of Rodriguez-Zuniga v. Garland involves a challenge to the Board of Immigration Appeals' (BIA) interpretation of the 'categorical approach' used to determine whether a prior conviction qualifies as an aggravated felony under the Immigration and Nationality Act, specifically regarding the definition of 'theft' and 'burglary' in the context of state statutes.",
      "source_date": "2022-10-15"
