{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "The Full Faith and Credit Clause of the U.S. Constitution (Article IV, Section 1) requires each state to recognize the public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of every other state.",
      "source_date": "1789-09-17"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "In the 1994 case of Baker v. General Motors Corp., the Supreme Court held that a state court judgment is entitled to full faith and credit in other states, and that a state cannot refuse to enforce a judgment from another state simply because it disagrees with the underlying reasoning or policy.",
      "source_date": "1998-06-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In the 1996 case of Franchise Tax Board of California v. Hyatt, the Supreme Court discussed the scope of the Full Faith and Credit Clause, noting that while states have some discretion, they cannot refuse to enforce judgments from sister states based on public policy objections.",
      "source_date": "1998-06-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court has historically applied the Full Faith and Credit Clause to family law matters, including custody and divorce decrees, requiring states to recognize valid judgments from other states even if the underlying facts or laws differ.",
      "source_date": "1970-06-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "In the case of V.L. v. E.L., the Alabama Court of Civil Appeals refused to recognize a same-sex adoption decree issued by a Georgia court, reasoning that the Georgia court lacked subject matter jurisdiction because Georgia law did not permit same-sex adoption at the time.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-18"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court granted certiorari in V.L. v. E.L. on October 5, 2015, to resolve a circuit split regarding whether the Full Faith and Credit Clause requires a state to recognize a judgment from another state when the rendering court's jurisdiction is challenged based on the public policy of the enforcing state.",
      "source_date": "2015-10-05"
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "fact": "In the 2015 case of Williams v. North Carolina, the Supreme Court established that a state court's determination of its own jurisdiction is generally entitled to full faith and credit in other states, unless the rendering court clearly lacked jurisdiction under its own laws.",
      "source_date": "1945-06-11"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "The central legal issue is whether the Full Faith and Credit Clause requires Alabama to recognize the Georgia adoption decree despite Alabama's public policy objections to same-sex adoption. Under the precedent set in Baker v. General Motors Corp. [2], a state cannot refuse to enforce a sister state's judgment simply because it disagrees with the underlying reasoning or policy. The Georgia court, having issued the decree, presumably had subject matter jurisdiction under Georgia law, and under the principle established in Williams v. North Carolina [7], Alabama is generally bound to respect that jurisdictional determination. The Alabama court's refusal to recognize the decree based on the lack of same-sex adoption rights in Georgia (which is not the enforcing state) or Alabama's own public policy conflicts with the mandatory nature of the Full Faith and Credit Clause as interpreted in Franchise Tax Board of California v. Hyatt [3]. The Supreme Court's grant of certiorari [6] indicates a high likelihood of resolving this in favor of the uniform application of the Clause, as the lower court's decision created a direct conflict with established precedent regarding the finality of judgments. Therefore, the Petitioner (V.L.) is highly likely to prevail based on the requirement that states must honor the judicial proceedings of sister states regardless of conflicting local public policies.",
  "probability_petitioner": 0.95
}

