{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "35 U.S.C. § 271(f)(1) imposes liability on a person who supplies from the United States components of a patented invention for combination outside the United States, specifically regarding the supply of 'all or a substantial portion of the components' of such invention.",
      "source_date": "1984-12-12"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "In Microsoft Corp. v. AT&T Corp. (2007), the Supreme Court held that a single software component, even if copied from the United States and installed on a foreign hard drive, does not constitute 'all or a substantial portion' of the components of a patented invention under § 271(f)(1) because the software itself is intangible and the physical copies were made abroad.",
      "source_date": "2007-05-29"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In Microsoft Corp. v. AT&T Corp. (2007), the Supreme Court further clarified that the phrase 'all or a substantial portion' refers to the number and importance of components, and that a single component, even if essential, is not a 'substantial portion' of the invention.",
      "source_date": "2007-05-29"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "The Federal Circuit, in Life Technologies Corp. v. Promega Corp. (2014), held that a single component (a primer) supplied from the United States could constitute a 'substantial portion' of the components of a patented invention under § 271(f)(1) if that component was essential to the invention's function.",
      "source_date": "2014-06-10"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court granted certiorari in Life Technologies Corp. v. Promega Corp. to resolve a circuit split regarding whether a single component can ever constitute a 'substantial portion' of the components of a patented invention under § 271(f)(1).",
      "source_date": "2016-03-21"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "In Microsoft Corp. v. AT&T Corp. (2007), the Court emphasized that the statutory language 'all or a substantial portion' implies a quantitative and qualitative assessment, but the Court in that case focused on the intangible nature of software and the fact that the copies were made abroad, leaving open the question of physical components.",
      "source_date": "2007-05-29"
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "fact": "The Federal Circuit in Life Technologies (2014) relied on the qualitative importance of the single component (a primer) to find liability, diverging from the quantitative approach suggested by the Microsoft decision's discussion of 'substantial portion'.",
      "source_date": "2014-06-10"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "The central legal question is whether a single component supplied from the United States can constitute a 'substantial portion' of the components of a patented invention under 35 U.S.C. § 271(f)(1). The Federal Circuit held that it could, based on the qualitative importance of the component [4][7]. However, this interpretation conflicts with the Supreme Court's precedent in Microsoft Corp. v. AT&T Corp., which suggested that a single component, even if essential, does not meet the statutory threshold [2][3]. In Microsoft, the Court analyzed the phrase 'all or a substantial portion' and indicated that a single component is not a substantial portion, focusing on the number of components rather than their individual importance [3]. The Supreme Court granted certiorari specifically to resolve this conflict between the Federal Circuit's qualitative approach and the quantitative implication of the Microsoft decision [5]. Given the Supreme Court's tendency to interpret patent statutes strictly and its prior rejection of extending liability to a single component in Microsoft, the Court is likely to reverse the Federal Circuit's decision, holding that a single component cannot constitute a 'substantial portion' of the components under § 271(f)(1).",
  "probability_petitioner": 0.85
