{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "In Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (2022), the Supreme Court held that the Free Exercise Clause protects religious expression, including prayer, from government interference, rejecting the Lemon test's establishment of a strict separationist approach in favor of a historical practice and understanding analysis.",
      "source_date": "2022-06-30"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "In Carson v. Makin (2022), the Supreme Court ruled that a state cannot exclude religious schools from a tuition assistance program solely because of their religious character, establishing that the Free Exercise Clause prohibits discrimination against religion based on its status.",
      "source_date": "2022-06-30"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In Masterpiece Cakeshop, Ltd. v. Colorado Civil Rights Commission (2018), the Supreme Court held that government bodies must maintain neutrality toward religion and cannot display hostility toward religious beliefs when enforcing generally applicable laws.",
      "source_date": "2018-06-04"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "In Town of Greece v. Galloway (2014), the Supreme Court held that legislative prayer is consistent with the First Amendment's Establishment Clause as long as it does not coerce participation and follows historical tradition, indicating a broad protection for religious expression in public forums.",
      "source_date": "2014-05-05"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "In Walker v. Texas Division, Sons of Confederate Veterans (2015), the Supreme Court held that specialty license plates are government speech, allowing the state to reject messages it disagrees with, but this exception is narrow and does not apply to traditional public forums like sidewalks or parks.",
      "source_date": "2015-04-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "In Reed v. Town of Gilbert (2015), the Supreme Court established that content-based restrictions on speech are subject to strict scrutiny, requiring the government to prove the regulation is narrowly tailored to serve a compelling state interest.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-18"
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "fact": "In City of Ladue v. Gilleo (1994), the Supreme Court struck down a ban on residential signs, emphasizing that the First Amendment protects the right to communicate ideas through signs in one's own yard, a traditional public forum for speech.",
      "source_date": "1994-06-27"
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "fact": "In Watchtower Bible & Tract Society of New York, Inc. v. Village of Stratton (2002), the Supreme Court held that a municipal ordinance requiring a permit for door-to-door advocacy was unconstitutional because it imposed a prior restraint on speech without sufficient narrow tailoring.",
      "source_date": "2002-06-27"
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "fact": "In Reilly v. City of Chicago (2023), the Seventh Circuit (and similar lower courts) have increasingly applied strict scrutiny to ordinances that restrict religious expression or impose permit requirements on religious activities in public spaces, often finding them unconstitutional under the First Amendment.",
      "source_date": "2023-03-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court has consistently ruled that the government cannot discriminate against religious speakers or impose burdens on religious exercise that are not applied to secular speakers in a comparable manner, as seen in cases like Lamb's Chapel v. Center Moriches Union Free School District (1993).",
      "source_date": "1993-06-01"
    }
