  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "In 2017, the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals ruled in Edmo v. Corizon, Inc. that the deliberate indifference standard of the Eighth Amendment applies to transgender prisoners seeking gender-affirming surgery, and that the district court's finding that the prison officials were deliberately indifferent was supported by the record.",
      "source_date": "2017-09-28"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court of the United States has established in Estelle v. Gamble (1976) that deliberate indifference to serious medical needs of prisoners constitutes cruel and unusual punishment under the Eighth Amendment.",
      "source_date": "1976-01-12"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In 2018, the Ninth Circuit denied a petition for rehearing en banc in Edmo v. Corizon, Inc., with a dissenting opinion arguing that the majority's decision expanded the definition of serious medical needs beyond established precedent.",
      "source_date": "2018-03-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "The Supreme Court granted certiorari in Edmo v. Corizon, Inc. on October 1, 2018, to review the Ninth Circuit's decision regarding the standard for deliberate indifference in the context of gender dysphoria treatment.",
      "source_date": "2018-10-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "Prior to 2019, the Supreme Court had not issued a definitive ruling on whether gender dysphoria constitutes a serious medical need requiring specific treatment like surgery under the Eighth Amendment, though lower courts were increasingly recognizing it as such.",
      "source_date": "2018-12-01"
    },
