  "reasoning": "The case of Clark v. Coupe centers on whether law enforcement officers are entitled to qualified immunity for alleged excessive force. Under the precedent set in Harlow v. Fitzgerald [3], officials are shielded unless they violate clearly established rights. The Supreme Court has consistently applied this standard strictly, particularly in cases involving split-second decisions, as seen in Mullenix v. Luna [5] and Kisela v. Hughes [6]. In Kisela, the Court emphasized that the law must be particularized to the specific facts, and in Mullenix, it reversed lower courts that failed to apply this high bar. Furthermore, Pearson v. Callahan [1] allows courts to skip the constitutional violation question and decide solely on whether the right was clearly established, which often leads to immunity grants if the specific conduct hasn't been previously ruled unconstitutional in a nearly identical context. Given the trend in the 2015-2019 term where the Court reversed lower courts for failing to grant immunity in ambiguous force situations [6], [7], and the general deference to officers in fact-specific scenarios, the Petitioner faces a significant hurdle in proving the law was clearly established at the time of the incident. While the Petitioner argues the force was excessive, the precedent suggests the Court will likely find that no prior case placed the constitutionality of such specific conduct beyond debate, thus granting immunity.",
